description:
Owner 2026-10-27: alongside the cloud enterprise API (BYOK), explore an on-device model for offline interview support, English first.
Why: works offline; answers never leave the phone (no processor, simpler consent).
Spike plan:
- candidates: Gemma 3n E2B/E4B, Llama 3.2 1B/3B, Qwen2.5 1.5B/3B, Phi-4-mini (quantised 4-bit);
- runtimes for Expo/React Native Android: Google AI Edge LiteRT-LM / MediaPipe LLM Inference, llama.cpp via llama.rn, ExecuTorch (react-native-executorch);
- device: test Samsung SM-E146B (6 GB; MemTotal 5.3 GB) is the coder baseline; interviewers' phones unknown — confirm their RAM;
- measure: model load time, tokens/s, peak RAM, battery, model download size (download on Wi-Fi, never bundled in the APK; APK builds on CI only);
- task quality on WHO VA logic: missing follow-ups, contradictions, narrative-vs-answers mismatch; compare with rule-only checks and the cloud model on the same de-identified test interviews;
- licensing of model weights for this use.
Outcome: a short report with a go/no-go per device class; the rule-based checks ship regardless.

notes:
Owner 2026-10-27: Flutter or Kotlin allowed if faster. Decision for the spike: keep the Expo app; implement inference as a Kotlin Expo native module (Expo Modules API) calling LiteRT-LM / MediaPipe LLM Inference (GPU/NPU delegates) on a background thread; JS passes context and receives suggestions. Flutter gives no inference gain (same native runtimes) and would mean rewriting Collect. Measure bridge overhead in the spike; reconsider full native only if it is a bottleneck.
Owner 2026-10-27: edge model under 600M parameters. Candidates: Gemma 3 270M (built for task-specific fine-tuning), SmolLM2-135M/360M-Instruct, Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct, MobileLLM (125M-350M); Qwen3-0.6B only as the ceiling. At 4-bit: ~150-350 MB download, <1 GB RAM, so it fits low-end interviewer phones. Expect weak open-ended reasoning: plan to fine-tune (e.g. Gemma 3 270M) on the narrow tasks — narrative-vs-answer mismatch, missing follow-up for WHO VA modules, thin-narrative flag — using de-identified/synthetic interviews; rules stay the backbone. Compare fine-tuned small model vs rules vs cloud on the same test set.
Owner 2026-10-27: under 100M parameters. That rules out generative LLMs (the smallest useful ones are 135M+), so the edge path becomes small encoder classifiers, which suit the narrow tasks better anyway:
- multi-label 'narrative mentions X' detection (fever, cough, injury, pregnancy, etc., mapped to WHO VA modules) and a thin-narrative score;
- candidates: MiniLM-L6/L12 (22M/33M), TinyBERT (14M), MobileBERT (25M), ELECTRA-small (14M), DistilBERT (66M); Indic later via IndicBERT (ALBERT ~33M) or multilingual MiniLM;
- int8 export via LiteRT or ONNX Runtime Mobile from the Kotlin module; ~15-60 MB, milliseconds per narrative, runs on any phone;
- no text generation on device: the follow-up prompt text comes from fixed templates keyed by the detected label and the unanswered WHO questions (auditable, translatable, no hallucination);
- train on de-identified coder-rated narratives plus labelled symptom mentions; quality gate per label (precision/recall), negation handled explicitly ('no fever').
Owner 2026-10-27: use distillation. Teacher labels, small student learns:
- teacher options, in order of data safety: (a) a larger open model on the local narrative workstation (digitva-sxg9; data stays in-house); (b) the cloud enterprise model with a project's own key (BYOK) on de-identified narratives only, under the narrative/interview-support consent purpose; (c) the cloud model generating synthetic VA narratives with known labels (no real data at all) to cover rare causes and negations;
- student: the <100M encoder classifier (MiniLM/TinyBERT/MobileBERT), trained on teacher soft labels (probabilities) plus coder NQA ratings as ground truth for the thin-narrative head;
- guard against teacher errors: a human-reviewed gold set per language never used for training; report student vs teacher vs gold; release only labels whose student precision/recall pass the gate;
- record teacher model/version and data source per training run (reproducible).
Owner 2026-10-27: runs on the dedicated Apple-silicon Mac mini support-model host (outbound-only machine account, leases work, pushes versioned results to the server; MLX/llama.cpp Metal/PyTorch MPS/R arm64; launchd services; heartbeat). Policy: docs/policy/narrative-ai-processing.md 'The support-model host'.
Owner 2026-10-27: host may be the Mac mini OR a Linux NVIDIA GPU workstation/server (Docker + NVIDIA Container Toolkit, vLLM/llama.cpp CUDA, PyTorch CUDA, R); same job contract on both, swappable.

description:
Owner 2026-10-27. During a DigitVA Collect interview, use the answers given so far (and the narrative, once de-identified) to flag inconsistencies, missing key symptoms and thin narratives, and to suggest follow-up questions the interviewer should ask (e.g. narrative mentions fever but fever questions skipped; maternal death suspected but pregnancy questions unanswered; injury mentioned without mechanism).
Design questions to settle first (policy before code):
- where it runs: on-device or local workstation model (offline Collect needs on-device rules); rule-based checks from WHO VA logic first, model suggestions second;
- data use: interview answers and narrative are personal data; no external AI unless policy and consent allow (see digitva-sxg9 narrative AI policy, consent item 7);
- suggestions only: never auto-fill or change answers; the interviewer decides; every suggestion shown and acted on is logged (no answer text in logs);
- measure benefit: completeness, NQA scores, coder agreement before vs after.
Related: digitva-sxg9 (narrative AI), digitva-r9lk (equity extension).

notes:
Owner 2026-10-27: the interview-support model runs in the cloud through an enterprise AI API (no training on data, zero data retention, DPA; per-project key via digitva-6383). Consequences to settle in its policy:
(1) consent: the default seed item 7 promises 'not sent to any outside company', so interview support needs its own purpose and wording naming an external AI processor; it cannot rely on item 7;
(2) minimisation: send only the answers and the de-identified narrative needed for the check, never the identity fields (names, phones, address, ids) — server-side pseudonymiser first;
(3) residency: per project — Gemini on Vertex asia-south1 if available, Claude region per enterprise terms; projects that require in-India processing restrict the provider list;
(4) online only: offline Collect shows nothing (or rule-only checks), no queued sending of answers;
(5) suggestions only, logged without answer text; provider TLS already X25519MLKEM768.
Owner 2026-10-27: interview support uses the project's own key (BYOK, digitva-6383); no platform-key fallback for this feature, so a project without its own enterprise key has interview support off.
